  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}a+3b=-6 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}a-\\frac{1}{3}b=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}a+3b=-6 & |\\times 2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}a-\\frac{1}{3}b=9 & |\\times 3\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}-5a+6b=-12 \\\\ 5a-b=27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $a$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{5a}+6b & =-12 \\\\ \\cancel{5a}-b & =27 \\\\ (6b-b) & =(-12+27) \\\\ 5b & =15 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $b=3$ into the second equation to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-b & =27 \\\\ 5a-3 & =27 \\\\ 5a & =30 \\\\ a & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $a=6,\\,b=3.$",
